One more abstraction occurs to me.

You could think of the Pantheon as having three constitutional layers:

Ontology
↓
Verification
↓
Enforcement

* Ontology says what the world is.
* Verification asks whether reality matches the ontology.
* Enforcement decides what to do if it doesn’t.

Those three together form what I’d call the constitutional layer. Everything else—daemons, translators, renderers, simulations—operates beneath it.

I also like one compact maxim for the repo:

Ontology decides what may exist. Verification decides what is true. Enforcement decides what changes.

That separation keeps responsibilities crisp and makes the system easier to evolve without blurring the roles.